    <body>
        <segment id="1" parent="12" relname="background">Efficiency and flexibility </segment>
        <segment id="2" parent="15" relname="span">It is important when designing a website or software product to keep in mind that users often look for flexibility,</segment>
        <segment id="3" parent="2" relname="elaboration">there should be several ways of doing the same the task easily.</segment>
        <segment id="4" parent="16" relname="span">Flexibility also ties in with efficiency.</segment>
        <segment id="5" parent="6" relname="condition">If a design is flexible and allows the user to perform one task in several ways</segment>
        <segment id="6" parent="17" relname="span">most likely the time taken to complete a task will be smaller.</segment>
        <segment id="7" parent="16" relname="elaboration">Efficiency can be measured by the time taken for a user to complete a task.</segment>
        <segment id="8" parent="19" relname="span">The goal of designing a product that is flexible, efficient, and attractive for a user ties in with increasing the satisfaction level for the user when using the product or surfing the website.</segment>
        <segment id="9" parent="14" relname="span">One of the best ways to increase user satisfaction is to make the make the design easy to learn to use.</segment>
        <segment id="10" parent="9" relname="volitional-cause">Users usually find an easy to use design more efficient and therefore will readily use the product.</segment>
        <group id="11" type="span"/>
        <group id="12" type="span" parent="11" relname="span"/>
        <group id="13" type="span" parent="12" relname="span"/>
        <group id="14" type="span" parent="8" relname="means"/>
        <group id="15" type="span" parent="13" relname="span"/>
